+# The Daily Grind account UI
+
+A static, responsive account security page. Open it through the existing static hosting setup. No dependencies or build step are required.
+
+The default account is an explicitly labeled preview. Its three sample passkeys can be renamed or removed; changes persist in local storage. Sample credentials never trigger authenticator signals. No real authentication or server-side account storage existed in the original project.
+
+## Connect an authenticated account API
+
+Set this configuration before `app.js` loads:
+
+```html
+<script>
+  window.dailyGrindAccount = {
+    apiBase: '/api/account/passkeys',
+    csrfToken: 'SERVER_GENERATED_CSRF_TOKEN'
+  };
+</script>
+```
+
+The client sends same-origin session credentials and the configured `X-CSRF-Token`. The backend must authenticate the customer, enforce CSRF checks and ownership on every operation, validate names, and guard against account lockout when deleting the last usable sign-in method. Do not populate this configuration from user-controlled content.
+
+Expected endpoints:
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`: returns the snapshot below, scoped to the signed-in customer.
+- `PATCH /api/account/passkeys/:id`: accepts `{ "name": "My laptop" }`; returns `204` after saving the account label.
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:id`: returns `204` only after revoking the credential on the server.
+
+```json
+{
+  "rpId": "your-production-domain.example",
+  "userId": "BASE64URL_WEBAUTHN_USER_HANDLE",
+  "passkeys": [
+    {
+      "id": "SERVER_RECORD_ID",
+      "credentialId": "BASE64URL_CREDENTIAL_ID",
+      "name": "MacBook Pro",
+      "kind": "laptop",
+      "provider": "iCloud Keychain",
+      "created": "Oct 12, 2026",
+      "lastUsed": "Today",
+      "current": true
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+`kind` supports `laptop`, `phone`, and `key`. The user ID is the original WebAuthn user handle, not an email or database account ID. Return `current` only when known; a passkey provider may sync credentials across multiple devices.
+
+After successful deletion, the client fetches a fresh server snapshot and calls `PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials` when supported. It falls back to `signalUnknownCredential`. These require a secure context and a valid relying-party domain. A successful signal reports notification, not guaranteed authenticator deletion. Missing support or a rejected signal produces guidance to remove the stale entry from the password manager. Failed deletion never signals the browser. A rename only changes the account label; WebAuthn does not expose an API to rename individual credentials in a password manager.
+
+The server and session integration, real registration, and actual sign-in are outside this static repository. Existing menu, order, and other account links retain their intended routes and require the corresponding site pages.
+
+## Checks
+
+Run `npm test` for persistence, validation, request handling, and authenticator signal tests. No server is started.
